  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1153 AM CDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nWater vapor satellite imagery and RAP upper air analysis at midday \nreveal a strong upper level ridge centered over the Desert Southwest \nwith a longwave trough downstream extending from eastern Canada \nsouthward towards Dixie Alley. At the surface, a broad lee cyclone \nfrom eastern CO through eastern MT will continue to deepen today as \nthe upper ridge flattens out and the flow across the central plains \nacquires a stronger westerly component. As a result, southerly winds \nwill intensify over the central plains, becoming sustained in the 15-\n25 mph range gusting to 35 mph. This along with 850-mb temperatures \nwarming by 3-5 degrees C under the influence of DNVA will engender a \nnoticeable uptick in temperatures this afternoon with highs in the \nmid to upper 90s. Later this evening/overnight, thunderstorms are \nnot expected across southwest KS as strong subsidence suppresses \nconvective activity, however a stray sub-severe thunderstorm or two \nemerging from eastern CO and/or northwest KS and impacting our \nnorthwest/north zones cannot be ruled out.\n\nDaytime Saturday, short range ensembles agree the upper level ridge \nwill begin to inch eastward, reaching the southern plains by 00Z \nSunday. The surface lee trough will follow while expanding, with its \ncenter shifting onto the northern plains. In response, winds \nacross southwest KS will veer to southwesterly and be sustained \nin the 15-25 mph range gusting to 35 mph once again. These \nmodestly downsloping winds will contribute to another jump in \nafternoon temperatures as highs peak in the 100-105 range. While\nno Heat Advisory is forthcoming, precautions should still be \ntaken to mitigate outdoor exposure.\n\nSunday through the end of the period, medium range ensembles agree \nthe upper level ridge will grow to encompass nearly the entire \nsouthern CONUS, save for far southern CA and adjacent areas. Under \npersistent synoptic-scale subsidence, southwest KS appears to be on \nthe heels of a prolonged hot and dry stretch as afternoon highs \nlinger in the 100-105 range with scant precipitation chances.",
